; MP/M page-zero definitions for uplm80's own references
;
; Under CP/M these addresses are absolute and the compiler may emit them as
; literals.  Under MP/M a process gets its own memory segment: the CLI loads a
; .PRL at segment_bottom+0100H and page zero lives at segment_bottom, so every
; one of these has to be relocated by the segment's base page when the program
; is loaded.
;
; Only a *resolved symbol reference* reaches the .PRL relocation bitmap, so
; they are published from their own module: a reference from inside the module
; that defines them would be assembled as a literal and never relocated.  This
; is the split DRI used, and the program's own page-zero names - MON1 to MON3,
; FCB, TBUFF, BOOT and the rest - come from DRI's PLM_WORK/X0100.ASM, linked
; with this.
;
; These are the compiler's own names, with its reserved `??' prefix, for the
; BDOS entry, the stack top and the warm-boot jump, so that a program declaring
; its own `bdos' or `boot' cannot capture them - UTIL7/DM.PLM declares a
; variable called `bdos'.

        .z80

??BDOS  equ     0005H
??BOOT  equ     0000H
??MAXB  equ     0006H
        public  ??BDOS, ??BOOT, ??MAXB

        end
